<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">George II of Great Britain</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1683">1683</a>–<a href="/years/1760">1760</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a> · Of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-hanover">House of Hanover</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">George II was king of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-great-britain">Great Britain</a> and Elector of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hanover">Hanover</a> from 1727 to 1760. He was the last British monarch to lead an army in battle, at Dettingen in 1743. In his reign <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> fought the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/war-of-the-austrian-succession">War of the Austrian Succession</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seven-years-war">Seven Years' War</a>, the colony of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/georgia">Georgia</a> was chartered in his name, and he founded the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/university-of-gottingen">University of Göttingen</a> in Hanover.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/george-ii-of-great-britain.jpg" alt="George II of Great Britain" width="320" height="528" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Thomas Hudson · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:George_II_by_Thomas_Hudson.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>George Augustus was born in the city of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hanover">Hanover</a>. His parents, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/george-i-of-great-britain">George Louis</a>, later George I, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sophia-dorothea-of-celle">Sophia Dorothea of Celle</a>, both committed adultery, and in 1694 the marriage was dissolved on the pretext that she had abandoned her husband. George spoke only French until the age of four, then learned German, English and Italian.</p><p>In June 1705 he visited the Ansbach court under the false name Monsieur de Busch to see Princess Caroline of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brandenburg-ansbach">Brandenburg-Ansbach</a>, and was so taken by her good character that he would think of nobody else. They married on 2 September 1705 at Herrenhausen Palace and had eight children. When his father became King in 1714, George was made <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prince-of-wales">Prince of Wales</a>; quarrelling with the King, he became associated with the opposition, and his residence at Leicester House was a meeting place for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/robert-walpole">Sir Robert Walpole</a> and Lord Townshend.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>George I died on 22 June 1727 and George II succeeded him as king and elector. He did not go to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> for the funeral, which the English took as proof of his fondness for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a>, and he suppressed his father's will, which tried to split the Hanoverian succession. He was crowned at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/westminster-abbey">Westminster Abbey</a> on 22 October 1727. He exercised little control over domestic policy, which Parliament largely controlled, and he spent twelve summers in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hanover">Hanover</a>.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> reopened hostilities with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> in 1739, in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/war-of-jenkins-ear">War of Jenkins' Ear</a>, which became part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/war-of-the-austrian-succession">War of the Austrian Succession</a>. George took part in the Battle of Dettingen and was the most recent British monarch to lead an army in battle. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jacobite-rising-of-1745">Jacobite rising of 1745</a> failed to depose the King. His son Frederick, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prince-of-wales">Prince of Wales</a>, supported the opposition and died in 1751. In 1756 a French invasion of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/menorca">Minorca</a> began the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seven-years-war">Seven Years' War</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>On 25 October 1760 George rose at six, drank hot chocolate, and was found on the floor of his closet; he was dead before his daughter Amelia arrived. At nearly seventy-seven he had lived longer than any English or British predecessor, and his grandson <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/george-iii">George III</a> succeeded him.</p><p>He donated the royal library to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/british-museum">British Museum</a> in 1757, founded the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/university-of-gottingen">University of Göttingen</a> in 1737. The province of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/georgia">Georgia</a>, chartered in 1732, was named after him. For two centuries historians tended to view him with disdain, concentrating on his mistresses, short temper and boorishness, but later scholars conclude that he exercised more influence in foreign policy and military appointments than was thought.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “George II of Great Britain” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote><b>George II</b> founded the University of Göttingen in 1734, which became a leading centre of learning.</blockquote><a href="/state/electorate-of-hanover">Electorate of Hanover</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1732 <b>George II</b> granted a charter to a board of Trustees led by James Oglethorpe for the land between the Savannah and Altamaha rivers.</blockquote><a href="/state/province-of-georgia">Province of Georgia</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Herrenhausen, in what is now <a href="/countries/germany">Germany</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/united-kingdom">the United Kingdom</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=52.39,9.69&amp;year=1733" aria-label="Herrenhausen on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of George II of Great Britain"><image href="/maps/globes/g000n60.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5503 5612h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5503 5612h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=52.39,9.69&amp;year=1733">What was here in 1733? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
